# ecvisor for wasmer: WASIX sockets plus the WASIX loader.
# `link-all --profile wasix`.
#
# ⚠️ NEVER A DEFAULT. The archive imports the `wasix_32v1` namespace, which only
# wasmer supplies; a module carrying it fails to instantiate anywhere else.
# `//runtime:loader_exclusion_test` and `//runtime:profile_exclusion_test` keep
# both halves of it out of the shipping archive.
#
# ⚠️ IT ALSO NEEDS `wasmer run --net`. Without that flag `sock_open` SUCCEEDS
# and `sock_bind` returns errno 58, so nothing reports a problem until the first
# bind. `internal/pipeline.runtimeArgs` passes it; a hand-rolled invocation must
# too.
#
# ❗ THE TWO HALVES ARE AT DIFFERENT STAGES, and the archive does not say so.
#   * The SOCKETS work. This is the first profile other than the default with
#     real external egress -- loopback is in-process only, browser needs a relay
#     server that does not exist.
#   * The LOADER does not, yet. WASIX refuses `dlopen` from an instance that is
#     not a dynamic library, so `--profile wasix` still answers errno 79 to
#     every `dlopen`. Making the SUPERVISOR one needs a PIC Rust std (nightly +
#     `rust-src` + `-Z build-std`), which the user deferred on 2026-08-24.
#     Its side modules already load; see `.agents/docs/TODO.md`.
#
# ⚠️ ITS NET BACKEND WAS `net-loopback` UNTIL WASIX SOCKETS EXISTED, and
# `loader_exclusion_test`'s attribution argument used to rest on that -- wasix
# and loopback differed only in the loader, so a symbol in one and not the other
# had to be the loader's. It now differs in both. The test's control moved with
# it rather than being dropped: see the WASIX ATTRIBUTION block there.

load("//bazel:ecvisor.bzl", "ecvisor_profile")

package(default_visibility = ["//visibility:public"])

ecvisor_profile(
    feature = "net-wasix",
    extra_features = ["load-wasix"],
)
